# Insure Nova Demo

What if you could understand a decades-old insurance application before making a single modernization decision?

What does the system do today?
What business capabilities are embedded within it?
What can be sunset or retired?
What needs to be retained, and what needs to be transformed?

And ultimately, what should the modern architecture look like?

Hi, welcome to the **Insure Nova Demo**.

Insure Nova is Hexaware’s **AI-led framework**, designed specifically for modernizing legacy insurance applications.

In a typical insurance landscape, you can have decades-old applications, including policy administration systems with millions of lines of code, complex business rules, tightly coupled components, and very limited documentation.

Before you can modernize, you first need answers to these fundamental questions.

What does my system currently do?

What are the business capabilities embedded in it?

What can be sunset or retired?

What needs to be retained, and what needs to be transformed?

And ultimately, what should my modern architecture look like?

**Insure Nova is here to bring clarity to these questions.**

It brings together AI agents, domain intelligence, and modernization frameworks, along with SME inputs through a **human-in-the-loop approach**.

The journey starts with understanding the legacy application, proceeds toward defining a modern target state, and ultimately results in a **domain-context-rich modernized application**.

Insure Nova uses a legacy modernization platform called **RapidX** and follows four distinct steps to achieve this.

Let me explain it through a demo.

For the purpose of this demonstration, we have taken an **RPG application** as input to RapidX.

This is the source code you're seeing on the screen, and this sample application represents a **policy aggregator** that helps users with their insurance policy needs.

Now, let's move to the **RapidX output**.

Once the source code is ingested, RapidX parses the application, and the first step is **Illuminate**.

Illuminate helps us understand and isolate the complexity within the legacy estate and uncover the underlying business logic.

To start with, RapidX provides an overall picture of the application and its hierarchy.

It also provides **module-level heuristics**, helping us identify modules with the highest number of rules, database calls, service calls, lines of code, and other key indicators.

This gives us an immediate understanding of where the complexity exists within the application and helps support modernization decisions.

To make the application easier to understand one level deeper, the **functional flows** are represented visually, showing how the different programs are connected.

For example, this particular functionality has around **five RPG files** being called.

It also provides a unified view of the number of **programs, tables, and rules** connected to this functionality, along with a simple, plain-English functional comprehension that even a **Business Analyst or Product Owner** can understand.

For example, this functionality is related to **processing insurance codes**.

It clearly identifies the different inputs required and takes the reader through the business process step by step.

The process begins by identifying whether the policy exists, then displays the codes based on different criteria, and finally requests a code from the selected third party.

The associated **business rules** are also captured as part of this understanding.

The components involved, including the **packages, procedures, and final outputs**, are also provided.

And to make the functionality easier to understand, RapidX presents it through a fictional example, helping readers connect the technical implementation with the business process.

Apart from the default view, we also have several other views for deeper analysis.

The **Algorithmic View** provides a step-by-step understanding of how the underlying logic is executed.

The **Risk and Rule View** helps us understand the business logic involved, when it is triggered, and what its purpose is.

And for understanding the logic behind database interactions, the **Data Flow View** provides a more granular view of how information moves through the application.

You'll also be able to get a similar set of outputs for the programs connected to this functionality.

RapidX can drill down one level further to the **method level**, where it provides a flowchart of the interactions, including the database node and the calculation node.

A simple flowchart from the beginning to the end of that method is available as a visual representation, along with the corresponding functional comprehension.

So far, we have looked at how the application currently works.

But you may be thinking — **what about the deeply buried business logic?**

If you're more concerned about **what is being implemented in the code**, rather than how it is implemented, then the **Rule Book** helps.

The Rule Book helps uncover the business rules embedded within the code while also identifying their complexity.

For each rule, it provides the **purpose, high-level steps, detailed algorithm, a dry run, and the associated code snippet**.

This helps you make informed decisions during modernization — whether a particular rule should be **kept, tweaked, or removed**.

RapidX can also help you download a comprehensive **Software Requirement Specification**, or SRS document.

The SRS acts as a living map and helps ensure that your team has a clear understanding of how the application behaves.

It provides a detailed comprehension of the application and brings together the understanding generated throughout the analysis.

In short, it documents the functionalities and business rules of the application so that everyone across the organization can speak the same language.

Now that we have sufficient **domain-rich artifacts**, we move to the second step, which is **Decouple**.

Here, RapidX identifies business domains and capabilities and determines how the target system can be designed into more manageable components.

Let's look at how RapidX has done it.

The **Capabilities** section of Blueprinting helps you identify what your system is currently good at.

For example, this application's capabilities include streamlining insurance operations, code processing, user authentication, code lifecycle, and more.

Within each capability, you will find associated **sub-capabilities and functionalities**.

Within the sub-capabilities, we also have a **Process Flow Visualizer**, which helps you understand the process end to end through a flowchart.

For example, the process starts with processing the email queue, extracting the code, combining all three codes, and then storing and updating the information.

RapidX can also intelligently identify certain **insights**.

For example, in this case, it identifies a **hard-coded loop**.

Think of it like a bus that stops at only three predetermined locations, even if passengers are waiting at twenty other stops.

These are important insights that can come in handy while you are **reimagining your system**.

Another insight here is that the system is checking patterns sequentially instead of using a single pattern match.

For example, it checks whether the person's name is **John**, then checks whether it is **Jane**, instead of directly looking up the information in a directory.

Moving on, every sub-capability also has a detailed **PDD**.

This captures and organizes the existing business goals in a simple, structured way.

It includes detailed business workflow information, a flowchart, and **algorithmic comprehension**.

This is essentially the **process DNA of your system**.

The PDD can be downloaded, and it can also be edited as per your requirements.

Next, we have the **AS-IS Business User Story** section.

This provides an understanding of the AS-IS functionality and how the business has been delivering value to multiple actors, depending on the use case.

In this case, the **insurance underwriter** is the user persona.

The user story captures what the actor wants, why they want it, and how the functionality delivers value.

Every user story includes the **business value**, along with the **acceptance criteria and boundary conditions**.

We also have the **Blueprinting Assistant**, which works like an SME chatbot.

With the Blueprinting Assistant, you can interact with the system, ask questions, and make informed decisions for the target-state blueprint.

SMEs can also use the Blueprinting Assistant to **brainstorm solution options**.

For example, let's say there is a new mandate where every customer interaction, quote, and insurer handoff must be digitally traceable and auditable.

We can ask the Blueprinting Assistant:

What changes should I make to the current application?

Which flows will be affected?

Which business roles will be affected?

And how should the architecture look?

The Blueprinting Assistant analyzes the current system context and provides a detailed analysis of the **current system architecture**, the changes required for digital traceability, and what needs to be added.

It also identifies which **business rules and flows are affected**.

And finally, it proposes the **target architecture**, along with an **implementation roadmap**.

This is where you get a lot of assistance, while also benefiting from domain-rich knowledge.

The assistant has the contextual business knowledge of your existing application and uses that understanding to suggest how you can proceed toward the target state.

This platform is more than just visualization.

It is your **knowledge companion**, giving SMEs everything they need to analyze, optimize, and reimagine their applications with confidence.

Now we move to the final step — the **modernization** part.

We call this stage **Reinvent**, because this step isn't just about writing code.

It is about using all the understanding we have gathered so far to create a modern insurance platform.

The SMEs can refer to the artifacts generated during the earlier steps and can also use the **Blueprinting Assistant** to define the future-state approach.

They can perform additional analysis to generate custom outputs that help support important design decisions.

All of these outputs — including the **architecture, PDD inputs, service definitions, and tech-stack mandates** — are then fed into the **RapidX Agentic Pipeline**.

Here, the expert guides the AI in making decisions that suit the specific application modernization requirements.

Finally, we get the code in the **TO-BE, or target technology stack**, and the application can now be modernized.

To conclude, **Insure Nova is a structured, AI-intelligence-driven journey from a legacy insurance environment to a modernized insurance application.**

Thank you.
